---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 15: The Gripper - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 15: The Gripper</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">Servo number four — and the mode machine grows up</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.2); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 10px; display: inline-block;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">🤏 The two-finger gripper comes alive — the arm can finally DO something</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Hardware</h3>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Wire the fourth servo: gripper on pin 5</li>
                        <li>How one servo drives two fingers through a linkage</li>
                        <li>Four SG90s on the regulated 5V/3A+ supply</li>
                        <li>Calibrate a gripper's own open/close range</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Software</h3>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Graduate the mode machine: bool → int + modulo</li>
                        <li>A name table for display strings</li>
                        <li>Per-joint angle limits (GRIP_OPEN / GRIP_CLOSED)</li>
                        <li>Homing that writes servos immediately</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: The mechanical story -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🤏 Section 1: One Servo, Two Fingers</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The gripper has been riding at the end of the forearm since Lesson 14, dead weight. Now its servo gets wired. Look at the mechanism: a typical two-finger gripper uses <strong>one</strong> servo driving a small gear or linkage that opens and closes <strong>both</strong> fingers together:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <pre style="margin: 0; color: #4a5568; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.8; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;"><code>{`servo horn rotates
        │
   drive gear / linkage
        │
   ┌────┴────┐
finger    finger      <-- both move together
   └────┬────┘
      grip!`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Why one channel is enough</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    The linkage forces the fingers to move symmetrically — you can't open one without the other. Mechanically that's brilliant: <strong>the hardware collapses two fingers into one degree of freedom</strong>, so one servo and one control channel do the whole job. Good mechanisms shrink the software problem.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pin Map -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📌 Section 2: Pin Map &amp; Wiring</h2>
            
            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Complete robot-arm pin map</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>OLED SDA / SCL:</strong> A4 / A5 (I2C bus)</li>
                    <li><strong>Joystick VRx / VRy / SW:</strong> A0 / A1 / D2</li>
                    <li><strong>Mode button:</strong> A2 — now cycles through <em>three</em> control pairs</li>
                    <li><strong>Buzzer (optional):</strong> pin 8</li>
                    <li><strong>Base servo:</strong> pin 9</li>
                    <li><strong>Shoulder servo:</strong> pin 10</li>
                    <li><strong>Elbow servo:</strong> pin 11</li>
                    <li><strong>Gripper servo:</strong> pin 5 — <em>new</em> (Lesson 14 told you it was free!)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Power check: you already prepared for this</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Lesson 14's supply recommendation — <strong>regulated 5V, around 3A or more</strong> — was sized for exactly this moment: four SG90s can spike past 2.5A if several stall at once. If you're still on the Uno's 5V pin, this is where it fails for good. Servo power rails on the external supply, <strong>grounds shared with the Arduino</strong>. Same rule as always: signals float without a common ground.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef3c7; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b;">
                <h4 style="color: #92400e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">💡 Find YOUR gripper's range first</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Before uploading, turn the gripper horn by hand (power off) and watch how far the fingers travel. Our sketch uses <code>GRIP_OPEN = 40</code> and <code>GRIP_CLOSED = 110</code> as starting guesses — every gripper's linkage is different. If yours stalls buzzing against its mechanical stop, <em>narrow the range in code, never force it wider</em>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The control problem -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎮 Section 3: Four Joints, Still Two Axes</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Last lesson we squeezed 3 joints onto 2 axes with a boolean mode. Booleans hold exactly two states — that trick is now maxed out. With the gripper we need <strong>three</strong> control pairs. Quiz 14 already asked how; here's the answer made real:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <pre style="margin: 0; color: #4a5568; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.8; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;"><code>{`mode 0 = BASE+SHLDR   (default)     X -> base     Y -> shoulder
mode 1 = BASE+ELBOW                 X -> base     Y -> elbow
mode 2 = BASE+GRIP                  X -> base     Y -> gripper open/close

A2 button press -> mode = (mode + 1) % 3   wraps back to 0`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The boolean <code>elbowMode</code> becomes an <code>int armMode</code>, and the toggle becomes the modulo operator — <code>%</code> wraps the counter back to 0 after the last mode. That's a complete little <strong>state machine</strong>: the same shape industrial mode selectors use.
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`int armMode = 0;                    // 0=BASE+SHLDR  1=BASE+ELBOW  2=BASE+GRIP
const int NUM_MODES = 3;

// Display name for each mode — an index, not an if-chain
const char* modeName[] = { "BASE+SHLDR", "BASE+ELBOW", "BASE+GRIP" };

// Edge-detect the A2 button once per loop pass
int btnState = digitalRead(modeButton);
if (btnState == LOW && lastBtnState == HIGH) {
    armMode = (armMode + 1) % NUM_MODES;   // cycle 0->1->2->0
    updateDisplay();                        // mode is safety info - show NOW
}
lastBtnState = btnState;`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #ecfdf5; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #059669;">
                <h4 style="color: #065f46; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🔍 Why the name table matters</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    <code>modeName[armMode]</code> replaces a ternary chain — with two modes a ternary was fine, with three it's ugly, with five it's a bug farm. An array lookup scales to however many modes you add. Same trick the pros use for state names in debug output.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Display update -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📺 Section 4: Four Angles on One Screen</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Row 2 now carries two readouts — elbow on the left, gripper on the right. Same pixel budget, same discipline:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`void updateDisplay() {
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);

    // Title + armed pair (yellow band)
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 0);
    display.print("ARM STATUS  ");
    display.print(modeName[armMode]);

    // Row 1: base & shoulder
    display.setTextSize(2);
    display.setCursor(0, 16);
    display.print("B:");
    printAngle(baseAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 16);
    display.print("S:");
    printAngle(shoulderAngle);

    // Row 2: elbow & gripper
    display.setCursor(0, 36);
    display.print("E:");
    printAngle(elbowAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 36);
    display.print("G:");
    printAngle(gripAngle);

    // Footer: live stick deflection
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 56);
    display.print("dX:");
    display.print(lastDX);
    display.print("  dY:");
    display.print(lastDY);

    display.display();
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Complete sketch -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🧩 Section 5: The Complete Sketch</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Four servos, three modes, still zero <code>delay()</code> calls in <code>loop()</code>. Notice the homing upgrade: the SW click now <em>writes all four servos immediately</em> instead of waiting for the armed joint's next write — otherwise a disarmed servo would hold its old angle after "home":
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`// ============================================================
// Lesson 15: The Gripper (servo #4 + 3-mode control pairs)
//   X -> base (pin 9)        always
//   Y -> shoulder (pin 10)   mode 0  BASE+SHLDR
//   Y -> elbow (pin 11)      mode 1  BASE+ELBOW
//   Y -> gripper (pin 5)     mode 2  BASE+GRIP
//   A2 button -> cycle mode  SW click -> home all four NOW
//   OLED -> B/S/E/G angles + armed pair
// ============================================================

#include <Servo.h>
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

Servo baseServo;
Servo shoulderServo;
Servo elbowServo;
Servo gripServo;

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64
#define OLED_ADDR 0x3C
Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, -1);

// ---- Pins ----
const int joyX       = A0;
const int joyY       = A1;
const int joySW      = 2;
const int modeButton = A2;

// ---- Servo state ----
int baseAngle     = 90;
int shoulderAngle = 90;
int elbowAngle    = 90;
const int MIN_ANGLE = 20;
const int MAX_ANGLE = 160;

// Gripper gets its OWN limits - different mechanism, different travel
const int GRIP_OPEN   = 40;
const int GRIP_CLOSED = 110;
int gripAngle = GRIP_OPEN;

// ---- Control-pair mode machine ----
int armMode = 0;                    // 0=BASE+SHLDR  1=BASE+ELBOW  2=BASE+GRIP
const int NUM_MODES = 3;
const char* modeName[] = { "BASE+SHLDR", "BASE+ELBOW", "BASE+GRIP" };

// ---- Joystick calibration ----
int centerX = 512;
int centerY = 512;
const int deadZone = 25;
int lastDX = 0;
int lastDY = 0;

// ---- Independent timers ----
unsigned long lastStepTime    = 0;
unsigned long lastDisplayTime = 0;
const int stepInterval    = 15;
const int displayInterval = 250;
const int maxStep = 4;

int lastSWState  = HIGH;
int lastBtnState = HIGH;

int signOf(int v) {
    return (v > 0) - (v < 0);
}

int stepFor(int deflection) {
    int d = abs(deflection);
    if (d < deadZone) return 0;
    return map(d, deadZone, 512, 1, maxStep);
}

void calibrateJoystick() {
    long sumX = 0, sumY = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        sumX += analogRead(joyX);
        sumY += analogRead(joyY);
        delay(10);
    }
    centerX = sumX / 50;
    centerY = sumY / 50;
}

void printAngle(int n) {
    if (n < 100) display.print(" ");
    display.print(n);
}

void updateDisplay() {
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);

    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 0);
    display.print("ARM STATUS  ");
    display.print(modeName[armMode]);

    display.setTextSize(2);
    display.setCursor(0, 16);
    display.print("B:");
    printAngle(baseAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 16);
    display.print("S:");
    printAngle(shoulderAngle);
    display.setCursor(0, 36);
    display.print("E:");
    printAngle(elbowAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 36);
    display.print("G:");
    printAngle(gripAngle);

    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 56);
    display.print("dX:");
    display.print(lastDX);
    display.print("  dY:");
    display.print(lastDY);

    display.display();
}

void homeAll() {
    baseAngle = shoulderAngle = elbowAngle = 90;
    gripAngle = GRIP_OPEN;
    // Write NOW - don't wait for the armed joint's next 15ms tick
    baseServo.write(baseAngle);
    shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
    elbowServo.write(elbowAngle);
    gripServo.write(gripAngle);
}

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
    baseServo.attach(9);
    shoulderServo.attach(10);
    elbowServo.attach(11);
    gripServo.attach(5);
    pinMode(joySW, INPUT_PULLUP);
    pinMode(modeButton, INPUT_PULLUP);
    homeAll();

    if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {
        Serial.println("OLED not found - check wiring/address");
        while (true);
    }
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 20);
    display.println("Calibrating...");
    display.println("Hands off stick!");
    display.display();

    calibrateJoystick();
    updateDisplay();
}

void loop() {
    // Job 1: timed servo motion (every 15 ms)
    if (millis() - lastStepTime >= stepInterval) {
        lastStepTime = millis();
        lastDX = analogRead(joyX) - centerX;
        lastDY = analogRead(joyY) - centerY;

        // X always drives the base
        baseAngle = constrain(baseAngle + signOf(lastDX) * stepFor(lastDX),
                              MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
        baseServo.write(baseAngle);

        // Y drives whichever vertical joint is armed
        if (armMode == 0) {
            shoulderAngle = constrain(shoulderAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY),
                                      MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
            shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
        } else if (armMode == 1) {
            elbowAngle = constrain(elbowAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY),
                                   MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
            elbowServo.write(elbowAngle);
        } else {
            // mode 2: gripper respects its OWN range, not the arm's
            gripAngle = constrain(gripAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY),
                                  GRIP_OPEN, GRIP_CLOSED);
            gripServo.write(gripAngle);
        }
    }

    // Job 2: timed screen refresh (every 250 ms)
    if (millis() - lastDisplayTime >= displayInterval) {
        lastDisplayTime = millis();
        updateDisplay();
    }

    // Job 3: buttons (every pass)
    int swState = digitalRead(joySW);
    if (swState == LOW && lastSWState == HIGH) {
        homeAll();          // writes all four immediately
    }
    lastSWState = swState;

    int btnState = digitalRead(modeButton);
    if (btnState == LOW && lastBtnState == HIGH) {
        armMode = (armMode + 1) % NUM_MODES;   // cycle 0->1->2->0
        updateDisplay();                        // instant feedback
    }
    lastBtnState = btnState;
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Tuning & challenges -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🔧 Section 6: Tuning and Next Steps</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Gripper buzzes or stalls</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">The code is asking for more travel than the linkage has. Narrow <code>GRIP_OPEN</code>/<code>GRIP_CLOSED</code> until the buzzing stops. Never solve a stall by forcing the range wider.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Grip direction feels backwards</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Swap the two constants' roles in your head: if Y-up should mean "close," flip the sign of the step or relabel which angle is open. Whatever feels right to the pilot wins.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Everything browns out on grab</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">A stalled gripper draws its maximum current continuously. If the OLED flickers when gripping, your supply sagged — check the 3A rating and shared ground again.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Wrong joint moves</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">You're in the wrong mode — glance at line 1 of the OLED before pushing the stick. Three modes is where mode awareness becomes a real pilot skill.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #0ea5e9;">
                <h4 style="color: #0c4a6e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🛠️ Put It Together — Challenges</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>The pick-and-place:</strong> the classic test. Drive to a small object, open, position, close, lift, rotate, lower, release. Count your mode switches — is the order what you expected?</li>
                    <li><strong>Highlight the armed joint:</strong> draw a <code>drawRect()</code> box around the armed readout (S, E, or G) instead of relying only on mode text.</li>
                    <li><strong>Audible modes:</strong> beep the buzzer on pin 8 a different count for each mode — 1, 2, 3 beeps. Pilots shouldn't have to look up.</li>
                    <li><strong>Gripper-on-click:</strong> try SW click toggling open/closed instead of homing (move homing to a long-press of A2). Which scheme do you like better for pick-and-place? Defend your choice.</li>
                    <li><strong>Gentle grip:</strong> when closing on an object, add a speed limit so the fingers don't slam. What changes in <code>stepFor()</code> or the gripper branch?</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="text-center; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <a href="/programming/quizzes32/lesson15" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 12px 24px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    Take Lesson 15 Quiz →
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Navigation -->
